WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
This test tube holds iron three plus ions

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.680
and thiocyanate ions, which join to make a

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
red ion.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.560
It has sat for an hour at the

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:11.520
same pale red, because joining and splitting are

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:13.440
running at equal rates.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.400
That is a dynamic equilibrium, like the sealed

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.800
flask of P C L five, because none

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.600
of the ions can escape from the solution.

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:23.360
Now we drop in a few crystals that

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.800
dissolve and add more iron three plus ions.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:28.000
Watch the colour.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:29.520
It deepens.

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.000
So how does a settled mixture respond when

00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:33.760
we change it, and which way does it

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.400
go?

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:37.840
Here is the tube at equilibrium, on the

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.160
same kind of made-up scale as the flask.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.040
Both rates are thirty, so the amounts are

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.320
steady: ten units of iron three plus, ten

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.040
of thiocyanate and ten of the red ion.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.640
Joining is the forward reaction and splitting is

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.600
the reverse.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.200
Now we add seven units more of iron

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.240
three plus, as solid, so the volume hardly

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:00.960
changes.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.680
Joining needs an iron ion to meet a

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:06.640
thiocyanate ion, and there are more iron ions

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:07.440
to meet.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.000
Joining speeds up in step with the iron

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.240
count, so seventeen tenths of thirty is fifty-one.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.960
Splitting depends only on how many red ions

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.200
there are, and we have not touched those,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.840
so the reverse rate stays at thirty.

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.560
At that moment nothing has reacted yet, but

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.560
the two rates no longer match.

00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:30.586
Now the forward reaction is faster, so more

00:01:30.647 --> 00:01:33.446
red ions form each second than split, and

00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:35.046
the amounts start to move.

00:01:35.687 --> 00:01:38.726
Iron and thiocyanate ions are used up, so

00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:40.246
the forward rate falls.

00:01:40.807 --> 00:01:43.286
Red ions build up, so the reverse rate

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:44.006
rises.

00:01:44.727 --> 00:01:47.286
A little later the forward rate is forty-three

00:01:47.607 --> 00:01:50.246
and the reverse rate is thirty-three, so the

00:01:50.447 --> 00:01:52.246
difference between the rates is closing.

00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:54.326
It is the same story as the P

00:01:54.407 --> 00:01:57.526
C L five flask: the faster rate falls,

00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:59.206
the slower rate rises.

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:03.193
Then the rates meet again at thirty-six.

00:02:03.474 --> 00:02:05.713
Both rates are higher than before, because there

00:02:05.794 --> 00:02:08.193
is more red ion to split, and more

00:02:08.274 --> 00:02:10.993
iron to meet the thiocyanate that is left.

00:02:11.314 --> 00:02:13.313
The red ion has gone from ten to

00:02:13.394 --> 00:02:16.313
twelve, so the tube is a deeper red.

00:02:16.674 --> 00:02:18.033
Now look at the iron.

00:02:18.274 --> 00:02:20.513
We added seven units and it is only

00:02:20.634 --> 00:02:23.473
down to fifteen, not back to ten.

00:02:23.594 --> 00:02:26.913
It partly cancelled our change, but never fully.

00:02:27.034 --> 00:02:29.153
We say the equilibrium has shifted to the

00:02:29.314 --> 00:02:32.113
right: the net reaction ran forward until the

00:02:32.314 --> 00:02:33.713
rates matched again.

00:02:33.754 --> 00:02:35.793
The new mixture is a new position of

00:02:35.874 --> 00:02:36.673
equilibrium.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.100
Here are the graphs.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.500
The iron jumps up the instant we add

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:44.300
it, then slides down to fifteen.

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:46.780
The red ion climbs to twelve.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.100
The forward rate steps up to fifty-one and

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:50.407
then falls.

00:02:50.433 --> 00:02:52.220
The reverse rate does not move at the

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:54.780
moment of the change, then rises, and the

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.660
two lines meet at thirty-six.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:00.420
So two things happen, in order.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.620
Adding the iron changed a concentration at once.

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.740
The reaction responded afterwards.

00:03:06.967 --> 00:03:09.206
In a fresh tube at equilibrium, we add

00:03:09.287 --> 00:03:11.366
a few drops of silver nitrate, so the

00:03:11.447 --> 00:03:12.966
volume hardly changes.

00:03:13.527 --> 00:03:16.566
Silver ions lock thiocyanate ions away as a

00:03:16.687 --> 00:03:17.686
white solid.

00:03:18.007 --> 00:03:20.006
We take out four of the ten units

00:03:20.047 --> 00:03:22.806
of thiocyanate, so six are left.

00:03:23.127 --> 00:03:25.846
Joining slows in step, so six tenths of

00:03:26.007 --> 00:03:27.686
thirty is eighteen.

00:03:28.007 --> 00:03:31.046
Splitting is untouched, so the reverse rate stays

00:03:31.087 --> 00:03:31.686
at thirty.

00:03:32.407 --> 00:03:35.606
Now reverse is the faster reaction, red ions

00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:37.766
split faster than they form, and the colour

00:03:37.927 --> 00:03:38.486
fades.

00:03:39.047 --> 00:03:41.046
The shift is to the left, toward what

00:03:41.127 --> 00:03:43.606
we took away, and again it only goes

00:03:43.767 --> 00:03:44.726
part of the way.

00:03:45.934 --> 00:03:48.533
This pattern is Le Chatelier's principle.

00:03:48.814 --> 00:03:52.253
When something disturbs an equilibrium, the mixture shifts

00:03:52.294 --> 00:03:55.413
in the direction that partly cancels the disturbance.

00:03:55.694 --> 00:03:58.013
In these examples, it is what the rates

00:03:58.174 --> 00:03:58.613
do.

00:03:58.894 --> 00:04:01.453
A change to a concentration alters one rate

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:04.413
first, then the rates close in.

00:04:04.534 --> 00:04:07.213
So adding a substance sends the shift away

00:04:07.294 --> 00:04:09.893
from it, and removing a substance sends the

00:04:09.934 --> 00:04:11.133
shift toward it.

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:14.333
A different kind of disturbance now.

00:04:14.894 --> 00:04:17.453
This tube holds pink cobalt ions in a

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:19.853
solution with lots of chloride ions.

00:04:20.494 --> 00:04:23.533
Pink cobalt ion plus chloride ions makes a

00:04:23.614 --> 00:04:25.213
blue ion plus water.

00:04:25.854 --> 00:04:28.573
An experiment shows that this reaction takes in

00:04:28.654 --> 00:04:30.813
heat, so it is endothermic.

00:04:31.374 --> 00:04:33.613
Its reverse gives out the same amount of

00:04:33.734 --> 00:04:35.773
heat, so it is exothermic.

00:04:36.414 --> 00:04:38.813
At room temperature the tube is between pink

00:04:38.894 --> 00:04:40.573
and blue, a purple.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:43.560
Now we warm it in a hot water

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:44.280
bath.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:47.400
That adds no ions and removes none, so

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:49.880
no concentration changes at that moment.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.920
Instead, heating changes the rates.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.040
Only collisions with enough energy make a reaction

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.560
happen, and heating makes those high-energy collisions much

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.280
more common.

00:05:00.760 --> 00:05:03.000
A reaction that takes in heat needs more

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:05.080
energy from each collision than the one that

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.320
gives it out, so it gains the most

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.840
from the extra heat.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.560
So both rates go up, but the heat-absorbing

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.000
one goes up more.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.920
On the same made-up scale, both rates were

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:17.960
thirty.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.760
Straight after heating, the forward rate is fifty

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.240
and the reverse is thirty-eight.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:25.960
Forward is faster, so the net reaction goes

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:27.960
right and blue ions form.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:31.000
As they build up, the forward rate falls

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.560
and the reverse rate rises until they meet

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:34.520
at forty-four.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.200
Both rates are higher than before, because it

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.480
is hotter, and the mixture has shifted to

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:41.880
the right, so the tube turns bluer.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.440
Taking in heat is the shift that partly

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:45.640
cancels the warming.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.440
Cool it in ice instead and both rates

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:51.400
drop, but the forward rate drops more, so

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:53.960
reverse wins and the tube turns pink.

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:56.286
Here is a shortcut.

00:05:56.567 --> 00:05:59.086
Write heat into the equation as if it

00:05:59.167 --> 00:06:00.206
were a chemical.

00:06:00.527 --> 00:06:03.566
This reaction takes in heat, so heat is

00:06:03.647 --> 00:06:07.886
a reactant: pink ion, plus chloride, plus heat,

00:06:08.007 --> 00:06:10.366
makes the blue ion and water.

00:06:10.487 --> 00:06:12.126
Where the heat goes depends on the

00:06:12.687 --> 00:06:13.299
reaction.

00:06:13.354 --> 00:06:16.046
If the forward reaction takes in heat, write

00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:17.566
heat on the left.

00:06:17.607 --> 00:06:19.646
If it gives out heat, write heat on

00:06:19.727 --> 00:06:22.526
the right, and then heating pushes the shift

00:06:22.687 --> 00:06:24.846
left, towards the reactants.

00:06:25.167 --> 00:06:27.566
Now treat heat like any other substance.

00:06:27.967 --> 00:06:30.206
Heat the tube and you have added heat,

00:06:30.287 --> 00:06:32.446
so the shift goes away from it.

00:06:32.527 --> 00:06:34.926
Cool it and you have taken heat away,

00:06:35.007 --> 00:06:36.726
so the shift goes toward it.

00:06:37.007 --> 00:06:38.606
It is a direction rule.

00:06:38.687 --> 00:06:40.766
The reason is still the rates.

00:06:41.967 --> 00:06:42.606
Your turn.

00:06:43.167 --> 00:06:45.966
In a sealed tube, colourless N two O

00:06:46.047 --> 00:06:48.766
four turns into brown N O two, and

00:06:48.847 --> 00:06:49.406
back again.

00:06:49.967 --> 00:06:53.246
The forward reaction takes in heat: delta H

00:06:53.367 --> 00:06:56.206
is plus fifty-seven kilojoules for each mole of

00:06:56.287 --> 00:06:57.726
N two O four.

00:06:57.807 --> 00:07:00.446
The tube starts at equilibrium in warm water

00:07:00.487 --> 00:07:03.486
at a steady temperature, and looks brown.

00:07:03.647 --> 00:07:06.046
One: a chemical removes some of the N

00:07:06.127 --> 00:07:08.846
O two, without changing the size or temperature

00:07:08.887 --> 00:07:10.206
of the tube.

00:07:10.327 --> 00:07:13.006
Which rate changes straight away, which way does

00:07:13.087 --> 00:07:15.326
the net reaction go, and what happens to

00:07:15.407 --> 00:07:17.806
the colour straight away, and then once the

00:07:17.847 --> 00:07:19.726
mixture has settled again?

00:07:19.887 --> 00:07:23.166
Two: instead, the original tube is moved into

00:07:23.327 --> 00:07:24.446
ice water.

00:07:24.567 --> 00:07:26.526
Which way does it shift, and what happens

00:07:26.607 --> 00:07:27.726
to the colour?

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:29.806
Pause the video if you'd like more time.

00:08:30.234 --> 00:08:30.633
One.

00:08:30.914 --> 00:08:33.913
Nitrogen dioxide is what joins in the reverse

00:08:34.194 --> 00:08:36.793
reaction, two N O two making N two

00:08:36.874 --> 00:08:37.713
O four.

00:08:37.994 --> 00:08:40.233
With less N O two around, the reverse

00:08:40.314 --> 00:08:42.873
rate drops straight away and the forward rate

00:08:42.954 --> 00:08:44.193
does not change.

00:08:44.474 --> 00:08:47.353
Forward is faster, so the net reaction goes

00:08:47.514 --> 00:08:47.913
forward.

00:08:48.474 --> 00:08:50.713
The brown fades at once, because N O

00:08:50.794 --> 00:08:54.313
two is the brown substance, then partly returns,

00:08:54.394 --> 00:08:56.633
but it stays paler than before, because the

00:08:56.714 --> 00:08:59.113
shift only partly cancels the change.

00:09:00.234 --> 00:09:00.793
Two.

00:09:00.874 --> 00:09:03.793
The forward reaction takes in heat, so cooling

00:09:03.914 --> 00:09:06.073
removes more of the hard collisions it relies

00:09:06.234 --> 00:09:07.833
on, and slows it more than the

00:09:08.394 --> 00:09:09.193
reverse.

00:09:09.434 --> 00:09:12.153
Reverse wins, the net reaction goes toward N

00:09:12.234 --> 00:09:14.553
two O four, and the tube becomes much

00:09:14.714 --> 00:09:15.593
paler.

00:09:15.674 --> 00:09:18.433
If you said the brown fully returns, remember

00:09:18.554 --> 00:09:20.633
the shift is only partial.

00:09:20.714 --> 00:09:22.553
If you said the forward rate dropped when

00:09:22.634 --> 00:09:25.073
the N O two was removed, check which

00:09:25.354 --> 00:09:27.193
reaction uses N O two.

00:09:27.354 --> 00:09:29.873
If you said cooling only slows the forward

00:09:30.154 --> 00:09:32.553
reaction, both slow, but the one that takes

00:09:32.714 --> 00:09:34.073
in heat slows more.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.580
So when a question disturbs an equilibrium, ask

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:41.620
what the change touched, a concentration or the

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:44.500
temperature, and which rate is now faster.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.620
The net reaction goes that way, only part

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.780
of the way back, until the rates match

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:50.180
again.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:54.060
Next: squeezing a gas mixture and adding a

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:54.900
catalyst.

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.300
Here are the key notes.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.700
Pause the video if you need more time

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:00.580
to copy them.
